From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 22:25:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] audio: wait for the primary input's frames instead of making
 them up

The primary input takes the frames its client asks for from a pipe that
an I/O thread fills with the buffers the device captures. When the pipe
was short it made silence up for the missing frames (a warning in the
log each time), and its sleep pacing made some up to keep a schedule of
its own. A recording thus held a gap of silence about that often, the
captured frames behind it came as late as the gap was long, and the
timestamps of the input could not be smooth.

Wait for the frames a bounded time, at least 100 ms and at most 250 ms,
and make silence up only when the device does not deliver within it.
The I/O thread wakes the wait each time it has written to the pipe, and
the wait ends when the thread is stopped. The device paces the input,
so the pacing of its own goes, with its members. The position of the
input stays its client's count and the time of the reply: when the
client keeps up, a reply follows the arrival of the frames it hands
over.
